<article class="env-remark" id="05C5">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05C5">Remark <span data-tag="05C5">10.66.10</span>.</a> Let $\varphi : R \to S$ be a ring map. Let $M$ be an $S$-module. Then it is not always the case that $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\varphi )(\text{WeakAss}_ S(M)) \subset \text{WeakAss}_ R(M)$ contrary to the case of associated primes (see Lemma <a href="/tag/05BW" data-tag="05BW">10.63.11</a>). An example is to consider the ring map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  R = k[x_1, x_2, x_3, \ldots ] \to S = k[x_1, x_2, x_3, \ldots , y_1, y_2, y_3, \ldots ]/ (x_1y_1, x_2y_2, x_3y_3, \ldots )  \]
</div>
  <p> and $M = S$. In this case $\mathfrak q = \sum x_ iS$ is a minimal prime of $S$, hence a weakly associated prime of $M = S$ (see Lemma <a href="/tag/05C4" data-tag="05C4">10.66.8</a>). But on the other hand, for any nonzero element of $S$ the annihilator in $R$ is finitely generated, and hence does not have radical equal to $R \cap \mathfrak q = (x_1, x_2, x_3, \ldots )$ (details omitted). </p>
</article>